---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { selectComponentEntries, groupByCategory } from '../lib/categories.ts';

// Sample-page compositions (ListReport/RecordDetail/Launcher/Dashboard/AppShell) render as
// "Patterns" below, not in this list, even though AppShell's own route still lives under
// /components/ (see docs-site/src/pages/components/[id].astro's comment for why) — the index
// groups by what a reader is looking for, not by URL shape.
const componentEntries = selectComponentEntries(await getCollection('docs'));
const { componentsByCategory, uncategorized } = groupByCategory(componentEntries);

const patterns = [
  { label: 'Launcher', href: '/patterns/launcher/' },
  { label: 'Dashboard', href: '/patterns/dashboard/' },
  { label: 'List report', href: '/patterns/list-report/' },
  { label: 'Record detail', href: '/patterns/record-detail/' },
  { label: 'App shell', href: '/components/appshell/' },
];
---
<Base title="Busy Office UI">
  <h1>Busy Office UI</h1>
  <p>Docs for <code>@busyoffice/design-system</code>, generated from the package's own <code>docs/*.md</code> files, tokens and example compositions.</p>
  <p><strong>What this is:</strong> a portable, reusable visual component package and reference page compositions for Busy Office applications — <strong>a UI dependency, not an ERP platform kernel or application implementation</strong>. Consumers own business behavior, data, navigation registration, permissions, and runtime integration; this repository owns components, styling compilation, fonts, documentation, and pure examples (<code>intent.md</code>).</p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p><code>@busyoffice/design-system</code> is a private package (<code>package.json</code>: <code>"private": true</code>) — not on the public npm registry. Hosts inside Busy Office Codex integrate it via a reviewed, pinned commit (see <code>AGENTS.md</code>'s repository-coordination process), then import from the documented subpaths (the main ones below — the full list, including the preview-only <code>/examples/*</code> sample hosts, is in <code>README.md</code>'s "Public imports"):</p>
  <pre><code>{`import { Button, Card, Text } from '@busyoffice/design-system';
import '@busyoffice/design-system/styles.css';
import { Shell } from '@busyoffice/design-system/shell';`}</code></pre>
  <p>Real component prop types remain authoritative — see <a href="/conventions/">Design conventions</a> for the full setup and styling idiom.</p>

  <h2>See it running</h2>
  <p>Every pattern below is a real, hydrated instance of the actual built package, not a screenshot — start with <a href="/patterns/list-report/">List report</a> (a filterable purchase-orders page) to see a complete ERP screen composed only from these components, or jump straight to a specific <a href="#components">component</a> or <a href="#patterns">pattern</a> below.</p>

  <h2>Foundations</h2>
  <h3>Design System</h3>
  <p>Tokens, themes and density — the ambient system every component reads from.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/tokens/">Tokens</a> — color (light/dark), spacing and type scale</li>
    <li><a href="/components/theme/">Theme</a> — light/dark/system theming</li>
    <li><a href="/density/">Density</a> — compact/comfortable/spacious tiers</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Base Styles</h3>
  <p><a href="/base-styles/">Elevation, radius, glass, motion and the icon set</a> — the visual primitives every component is built from.</p>

  <h2 id="components">Components</h2>
  <p><a href="/components/">Browse the components gallery</a> — every component below, live and filterable by category in one grid, not just linked one at a time.</p>
  {componentsByCategory.map((group) => (
    <>
      <h3>{group.label}</h3>
      <ul>
        {group.entries.map((entry) => (
          <li><a href={`/components/${entry.id}/`}>{entry.id}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </>
  ))}
  {uncategorized.length > 0 && (
    <>
      <h3>Other</h3>
      <ul>
        {uncategorized.map((entry) => (
          <li><a href={`/components/${entry.id}/`}>{entry.id}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </>
  )}

  <h2 id="patterns">Patterns</h2>
  <p>Composed sample-page journeys, not single components — see each page's "Not for" note.</p>
  <ul>
    {patterns.map((pattern) => (
      <li><a href={pattern.href}>{pattern.label}</a></li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>Examples</h2>
  <p><a href="/examples/">The full examples gallery</a> — all 47 <code>examples/*.tsx</code> screens catalogued by module, including the entry/state screens (sign-in, session-expired, 404) that aren't reachable from inside the running app today.</p>

  <h2>Quality &amp; Verification</h2>
  <p><a href="/quality/">What actually verifies a change here</a> — the CI gate suite, the review process, disclosed known limitations, and each framework component's own M10 review score.</p>

  <h2>Build with AI</h2>
  <p><a href="/build-with-ai/">Scope, the two-way/one-way gate, and common mistakes</a> — for an agent or contributor about to make a change here.</p>

  <h2>Layouts</h2>
  <p><a href="/layouts/">Standard layout skeletons</a> — named structural patterns real screens already repeat (list+detail, record-detail two-column, KPI row), each a guideline with real consumer counts, not an exported component.</p>

  <h2>Conventions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/conventions/">Design conventions</a></li>
  </ul>
</Base>
